order: 45
---
short_label: I.45
---
red_highlight: yes
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To construct a parallelogram equal to a given rectilinear figure in a given rectilinear angle.
----
proof:

Let {ABCD} be the given rectilinear figure and {E} the given rectilinear angle.

It is required to construct a parallelogram equal to the rectilinear figure {ABCD} in the given angle {E}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="400" height="240" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.45 diagram" src="propI45.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Closes the application-of-areas run: any rectilinear figure becomes a
    // parallelogram in any angle. The diagonal DB cuts ABCD into two triangles;
    // I.42 turns the first into a parallelogram, I.44 applies the second onto
    // its side, and the long middle stretch proves the two line up into one
    // parallelogram (KH straight with HM, FG straight with GL, both by I.14).
    //
    // COLOUR CARRIES THE WHOLE ARGUMENT, and it is a sum this time rather than
    // a pair: ABCD splits into ABD (orange) + DBC (green); each becomes a
    // parallelogram of the SAME colour — FH orange, GM green; and the two
    // together are KFLM. So the given figure and the constructed parallelogram
    // are both cyan, and both are visibly orange + green. Slide 13 is where
    // that pays off: four regions on screen, matched in pairs by colour.
    //
    // Angle colours follow the same rule. Every angle the proof shows equal to
    // E is magenta — E itself, HKF, GHM and (via the alternate-angle step) HGF.
    // The two supplements added along the way, KHG and HGL, are equal to each
    // other, so they are both yellow. Radii pinned to 22: KHG/GHM are adjacent
    // at H and HGF/HGL adjacent at G, so #103 must not step them apart.
    //
    // euclid#138: helpers H'(350,190) and M'(390,190) are on-canvas, so the
    // drift here is only -15px horizontally. Nothing to work around.
    var GIV = ["A","B","C","D","ABCD","E","E1","E2","E3","E1E2","E2E3","angE"];
    var SPL = ["A","B","C","D","BD","ABD","DBC","E","E1","E2","E3","E1E2","E2E3","angE"];
    var B1  = ["A","B","C","D","BD","ABD","DBC","E","E1","E2","E3","E1E2","E2E3","angE",
               "K","F","G","H","KF","FG","GH","HK","KFGH","angFKH"];
    var B2  = B1.concat(["L","M","GL","LM","MH","KM","FL","HGLM","angGHM"]);
    var A1  = B2.concat(["angKHG"]);
    var A2  = A1.concat(["angHGF"]);
    var A3  = A2.concat(["angHGL"]);
    var PG  = ["A","B","C","D","BD","ABD","DBC","E","E1","E2","E3","E1E2","E2E3","angE",
               "K","F","G","H","L","M","KF","FG","GH","HK","GL","LM","MH","KM","FL",
               "angFKH","angGHM","angKHG","angHGF","angHGL","KFLM"];
    var DONE= ["A","B","C","D","ABCD","E","E1","E2","E3","E1E2","E2E3","angE",
               "K","F","G","H","L","M","KF","FG","GH","HK","GL","LM","MH","KM","FL",
               "angFKH","KFLM"];
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.45",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,60",
            "B;point;free;100,200",
            "C;point;free;150,80",
            "D;point;free;100,40",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            // The given figure, and the two triangles the diagonal cuts it into.
            "ABCD;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;0,0,0;cyan",
            "ABD;polygon;triangle;A,B,D;0;0;0;orange",
            "DBC;polygon;triangle;D,B,C;0;0;0;green",
            // The given angle E: two arms plus a label-only polygon.
            "E1;point;free;180,120;0",
            "E2;point;free;170,150;0",
            "E3;point;free;200,150;0",
            "E1E2;line;connect;E1,E2",
            "E2E3;line;connect;E2,E3",
            "E;polygon;triangle;E1,E2,E3;0,0,0;0;0;0",
            "K;point;free;230,190",
            "F;point;free;270,70",
            // FH = the I.42 parallelogram on KF equal to triangle ABD, angle HKF = E.
            "H';point;similar;F,K,E1,E2,E3;0;0",
            "KFGH;polygon;application;ABD,K,F,H';0;0;0;orange",
            "H;point;vertex;KFGH,4",
            "G;point;vertex;KFGH,3",
            // GM = the I.44 parallelogram applied to GH equal to triangle DBC,
            // angle GHM = E.
            "M';point;similar;G,H,E1,E2,E3;0;0",
            "HGLM;polygon;application;DBC,H,G,M';0;0;0;green",
            "M;point;vertex;HGLM,4",
            "L;point;vertex;HGLM,3",
            // The result: the two parallelograms joined. Equal to ABCD, so cyan.
            "KFLM;polygon;quadrilateral;K,F,L,M;0;0;0;cyan",
            // Every segment the proof names, drawn in its own right so the
            // outlines stand whichever region fills are gated off.
            "KF;line;connect;K,F",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G",
            "GH;line;connect;G,H",
            "HK;line;connect;H,K",
            "GL;line;connect;G,L",
            "LM;line;connect;L,M",
            "MH;line;connect;M,H",
            // KM and FL are the joined lines the proof establishes as straight;
            // already drawn by their halves, so hover targets only.
            "KM;line;connect;K,M;0;0;0",
            "FL;line;connect;F,L;0;0;0",
            // Angles. Magenta = shown equal to E; yellow = the two supplements,
            // which are equal to each other. Radii pinned so #103 leaves the
            // adjacent pairs at H and at G alone.
            "angE;sector;angleMarker;E2,E1,E3;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angFKH;sector;angleMarker;K,F,H,22;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angGHM;sector;angleMarker;H,G,M,22;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angHGF;sector;angleMarker;G,H,F,22;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angKHG;sector;angleMarker;H,K,G,22;0;0;yellow;yellow",
            "angHGL;sector;angleMarker;G,H,L,22;0;0;yellow;yellow"
        ],
        aliases: { "FH": "KFGH", "GM": "HGLM", "DB": "BD",
                   "HKF": "angFKH", "FKH": "angFKH", "FKM": "angFKH",
                   "GHM": "angGHM", "MHG": "angGHM",
                   "KHG": "angKHG", "HGF": "angHGF", "HGL": "angHGL",
                   "FK": "KF", "HG": "GH", "KH": "HK", "HM": "MH",
                   "ML": "LM", "MK": "KM", "LF": "FL", "GF": "FG", "LG": "GL" },
        // K and F are free points, so without this they would be auto-unioned
        // into every slide and stand on the canvas from slide 1 — before the
        // proof introduces them with the I.42 construction on slide 3.
        deferDraggables: ["K","F"],
        initiallyHidden: ["ABD","DBC","KFLM"],
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {ABCD} be the given rectilinear figure and {E} the given rectilinear angle. It is required to construct a parallelogram equal to {ABCD} in the given angle {E}.",
              visible: GIV,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","E","angE"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "Join {DB}. It cuts the figure into the triangles {ABD} and {DBC}.",
              visible: SPL,
              highlighted: ["BD","ABD","DBC"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BD",  name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "ABD", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "DBC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Construct the parallelogram {FH} equal to the triangle {ABD} in the angle {HKF} which equals {E}.",
              visible: B1,
              highlighted: ["KFGH","ABD","angFKH"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "KFGH",   name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "angFKH", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.42" }] },
            { text: "Apply the parallelogram {GM} equal to the triangle {DBC} to the straight line {GH} in the angle {GHM} which equals {E}.",
              visible: B2,
              highlighted: ["HGLM","DBC","angGHM","GH"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "HGLM",   name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "angGHM", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.44" }] },
            { text: "Since the angle {E} equals each of the angles {HKF} and {GHM}, therefore the angle {HKF} also equals the angle {GHM}.",
              visible: B2,
              highlighted: ["angE","angFKH","angGHM"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.1" }] },
            { text: "Add the angle {KHG} to each. Therefore the sum of the angles {FKH} and {KHG} equals the sum of the angles {KHG} and {GHM}.",
              visible: A1,
              highlighted: ["angKHG","angFKH","angGHM"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angKHG", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "But the sum of the angles {FKH} and {KHG} equals two right angles, therefore the sum of {KHG} and {GHM} also equals two right angles.",
              visible: A1,
              highlighted: ["angFKH","angKHG","angGHM"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.29" }, { ref: "C.N.1" }] },
            { text: "So with the straight line {GH}, at the point {H}, the two straight lines {KH} and {HM} make adjacent angles equal to two right angles — therefore {KH} is in a straight line with {HM}.",
              visible: A1,
              highlighted: ["HK","MH","KM"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.14" }] },
            { text: "Since the straight line {HG} falls upon the parallels {KM} and {FG}, the alternate angles {MHG} and {HGF} equal one another.",
              visible: A2,
              highlighted: ["angGHM","angHGF","GH"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angHGF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.29" }] },
            { text: "Add the angle {HGL} to each. Then the sum of {MHG} and {HGL} equals the sum of {HGF} and {HGL}.",
              visible: A3,
              highlighted: ["angHGL","angGHM","angHGF"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angHGL", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "But the sum of {MHG} and {HGL} equals two right angles, so the sum of {HGF} and {HGL} does too. Therefore {FG} is in a straight line with {GL}.",
              visible: A3,
              highlighted: ["angHGF","angHGL","FG","GL","FL"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.29" }, { ref: "C.N.1" }, { ref: "I.14" }] },
            { text: "Since {FK} is equal and parallel to {HG}, and {HG} to {ML}, therefore {KF} is equal and parallel to {ML}, and {KM} and {FL} join them at their ends. So {KFLM} is a parallelogram.",
              visible: PG,
              highlighted: ["KFLM","KM","FL"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "KFLM", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.34" }, { ref: "I.30" }, { ref: "C.N.1" }, { ref: "I.33" }] },
            { text: "Since the triangle {ABD} equals the parallelogram {FH}, and {DBC} equals {GM}, therefore the whole figure {ABCD} equals the whole parallelogram {KFLM}.",
              visible: A3,
              highlighted: ["ABD","KFGH","DBC","HGLM"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "So the parallelogram {KFLM} has been constructed equal to the given rectilinear figure {ABCD}, in the angle {FKM} which equals the given angle {E}.",
              visible: DONE,
              highlighted: ["KFLM","ABCD","angFKH"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.14":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI14/",
        "I.29":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI29/",
        "I.30":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI30/",
        "I.33":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI33/",
        "I.34":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "I.42":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI42/",
        "I.44":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI44/",
        "C.N.1":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn1/",
        "C.N.2":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Join {DB}. Construct the parallelogram {FH} equal to the triangle {ABD} in the angle {HKF|angFKH} which equals {E}. Apply the parallelogram {GM} equal to the triangle {DBC} to the straight line {GH} in the angle {GHM|angGHM} which equals {E}. [!just I.Post.1; I.42; I.44]

Since the angle {E} equals each of the angles {HKF|angFKH} and {GHM|angGHM}, therefore the angle {HKF|angFKH} also equals the angle {GHM|angGHM}. [!just C.N.1]

Add the angle {KHG|angKHG} to each. Therefore the sum of the angles {FKH|angFKH} and {KHG|angKHG} equals the sum of the angles {KHG|angKHG} and {GHM|angGHM}. [!just C.N.2]

But the sum of the angles {FKH|angFKH} and {KHG|angKHG} equals two right angles, therefore the sum of the angles {KHG|angKHG} and {GHM|angGHM} also equals two right angles. [!just I.29; C.N.1]

Thus, with a straight line {GH}, and at the point {H} on it, two straight lines {KH} and {HM} not lying on the same side make the adjacent angles together equal to two right angles, therefore {KH} is in a straight line with {HM}. [!just I.14]

Since the straight line {HG} falls upon the parallels {KM} and {FG}, therefore the alternate angles {MHG|angGHM} and {HGF|angHGF} equal one another. [!just I.29]

Add the angle {HGL|angHGL} to each. Then the sum of the angles {MHG|angGHM} and {HGL|angHGL} equals the sum of the angles {HGF|angHGF} and {HGL|angHGL}. [!just C.N.2]

But the sum of the angles {MHG|angGHM} and {HGL|angHGL} equals two right angles, therefore the sum of the angles {HGF|angHGF} and {HGL|angHGL} also equals two right angles. Therefore {FG} is in a straight line with {GL}. [!just I.29, C.N.1; I.14]

Since {FK} is equal and parallel to {HG}, and {HG} equal and parallel to {ML} also, therefore {KF} is also equal and parallel to {ML}, and the straight lines {KM} and {FL} join them at their ends. Therefore {KM} and {FL} are also equal and parallel. Therefore {KFLM} is a parallelogram. [!just I.34, I.30; C.N.1, I.33]

Since the triangle {ABD} equals the parallelogram {FH}, and {DBC} equals {GM}, therefore the whole rectilinear figure {ABCD} equals the whole parallelogram {KFLM}. [!just C.N.2]

Therefore the parallelogram {KFLM} has been constructed equal to the given rectilinear figure {ABCD} in the angle {FKM|angFKH} which equals the given angle {E}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

With this construction any rectilinear area can be applied to a line in an angle, that is, it can be transformed into a parallelogram with whatever angle you want and with one side whatever you want. That is a satisfactory solution to the question "what's the area of this figure?"

But the question "what's the area of a circle?" is not answered in the *Elements.* See the note on [squaring the circle](/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII14/#squaring) after proposition II.14 for more discussion of this question.

#### Use of Proposition 45

This construction is used in propositions @II.14, @VI.25, and @XI.32. Like many of the other constructions in Book I, it is used to make constructions in different planes as is done in XI.32.
